# 261. Graph Valid Tree

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/graph-valid-tree/
- **Topic:** Graph, DFS, Union-Find (DSU)
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Practiced

## 1. My Intuition

A graph is a valid tree if it is connected and contains no cycles.

I initially thought of solving this using two approaches:

1. DFS: Build an adjacency list and use a visited array to detect cycles.
2. DSU: Check whether the two endpoints of an edge already belong to the same component. If they do, adding that edge creates a cycle.

## 2. My Approach

### Approach 1: DFS

1. Build an undirected adjacency list.
2. Check whether the number of edges is exactly n - 1.
3. Start DFS from node 0.
4. Pass the parent node to avoid treating the edge back to the parent as a cycle.
5. If a visited neighbor is not the parent, a cycle exists.
6. Finally, check whether all nodes were visited.

### Approach 2: DSU

1. Initialize every node as its own parent.
2. Initialize the size of every component as 1.
3. Check whether the number of edges is n - 1.
4. For every edge, find the roots of both endpoints.
5. If both roots are equal, a cycle exists.
6. Otherwise, union the two components.
7. If no cycle is detected, the graph is a valid tree.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

Initially, I thought encountering an already visited node during DFS always meant a cycle.

However, in an undirected graph, DFS also encounters the parent node because edges work in both directions.

The correction is to pass the parent node and detect a cycle only when an already visited neighbor is not the parent.

## 4. What Clicked

A tree must satisfy two conditions:

- It must be connected.
- It must not contain cycles.

A useful property is:

A graph with n nodes is a tree if and only if it has exactly n - 1 edges and is acyclic.

This allows us to check the edge count first and then focus on cycle detection.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

### DFS Approach

    class Solution {
        public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {
            if (edges.length != n - 1) return false;

            List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
            }

            for (int[] e : edges) {
                adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);
                adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);
            }

            boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

            if (dfs(0, -1, adj, visited)) return false;

            for (boolean v : visited) {
                if (!v) return false;
            }

            return true;
        }

        boolean dfs(int node, int parent, List<List<Integer>> adj, boolean[] visited) {
            visited[node] = true;

            for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
                if (!visited[next]) {
                    if (dfs(next, node, adj, visited)) return true;
                } else if (next != parent) {
                    return true;
                }
            }

            return false;
        }
    }

### DSU Approach

    class Solution {
        int[] parent, size;

        int find(int x) {
            if (parent[x] != x) {
                parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
            }
            return parent[x];
        }

        boolean union(int u, int v) {
            int pu = find(u);
            int pv = find(v);

            if (pu == pv) return false;

            if (size[pu] < size[pv]) {
                parent[pu] = pv;
                size[pv] += size[pu];
            } else {
                parent[pv] = pu;
                size[pu] += size[pv];
            }

            return true;
        }

        public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {
            if (edges.length != n - 1) return false;

            parent = new int[n];
            size = new int[n];

            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                parent[i] = i;
                size[i] = 1;
            }

            for (int[] e : edges) {
                if (!union(e[0], e[1])) return false;
            }

            return true;
        }
    }

## 6. Complexity Analysis

### DFS

- Time Complexity: O(V + E)
- Space Complexity: O(V + E), including the adjacency list and visited array.

### DSU

- Time Complexity: O(V + E × α(V))
- Space Complexity: O(V)

## 7. Revision Notes

- A valid tree must be connected and acyclic.
- A tree with n nodes has exactly n - 1 edges.
- In undirected DFS, always account for the parent node.
- DSU detects cycles when both endpoints have the same root.
- Remember path compression and union by size.
